Orange dots are requests: payment events from the bank's switch, and staff using the web app. Blue dots are database queries.
Everything inside the orange box is one server: Nginx ends TLS and serves the web app; the API runs the mule core and the three new engines and returns a 0–100 score with reasons;
PostgreSQL holds alerts, cases, non-approved decisions and the audit chain; the graph refresher rebuilds the mule-ring map every 2 seconds so no payment waits for it; models/ holds
the learned weights and the downloaded IP lists.
The crossed-out box: a separate database server adds nothing for speed. The pilot's busiest minute is 30.4 decisions a second; the decision log took ≈ 88,889 durable row appends a
second on this machine (4.8, SQLite stand-in).
Outside the box: the bank's single sign-on and payment switch, a backup copy in India, and an optional language model that only writes case narratives and never touches a score.

The six boxes at the bottom are the busiest calls, with pilot volume per day. Orange = writes, blue = reads, purple = AI. All calls are in section 4.5.
Why not today's host: the public demo runs on Railway, which has no India region. RBI requires payment-system data to be stored only in India, so the bank pilot needs an Indian region
(D01). The demo uses synthetic data only.
