What this means: for the pilot, one machine is plenty, and the database is not the bottleneck. The work is in the per-payment scoring path (today it costs 46–108 ms a payment,
measured here) and in keeping genuine customers out of the review queue: at realistic fraud rates, a 0.5 % false-positive rate would put 3,469 genuine payments in front of
investigators every day.
