The brief's twelve steps map onto this picture: ingestion ( POST /risk/assess , the trusted POST /risk/events batch, the live stream, or Kafka at stage 2) → validation and enrichment
(typed schema, provenance stamp, profile and ring-map lookups) → feature extraction inside each engine → the mule core and the three new engines → aggregation (learned weights)
→ 0–100 score → explanation → band and action → feedback.

6.3 The four engines

Each engine returns a score from 0 to 1, a confidence, and a list of reasons. Every reason has a code, plain text, a strength and a kind: weak (context), strong (evidence) or decisive (a
confirmed fact). The same fact is counted in one engine only, so that "two engines agree" really means two independent things.

6.3.1 Mule network: the core E extended

The existing per-payment scorer ( services/realtime_scorer.py ) and its ring rules ( detection/ring_decision.py ) stay as they are and keep serving /score and the live mule stream.
The unified engine reuses the same functions through a ring map that the graph refresher rebuilds every 2 s, so a payment reads ring membership and evidence in O(1) instead of
recomputing them (46–108 ms today).

Ring core, not the whole community. Louvain puts a ring's victims in the same community as the ring, because they paid into it. In the simulated rings that diluted a 6-account ring into a
338-account community and hid it from the purity test. Before ring_is_flaggable runs, the ring map now removes every account that paid into the community, received nothing back
from it and shows no mule pattern of its own. It re-scores the remaining core with the same Louvain scoring rules (density, shared devices and IPs, young accounts). The excluded payers
stay reachable as one-hop contacts (BI02) and are never called ring members. A customer whose account was taken over and drained into a mule is a victim, not a mule.

Code  What it checks                                                                                             Kind
MN01  payer or payee carries its own mule evidence (multi-scale smurf score ≥ 0.30)                              strong, scaled by the evidence
MN02  payer or payee is in a ring whose core passes ring_is_flaggable (size ≥ 3, purity ≥ 30 %, strength ≥ 0.5)  decisive
MN03  payee is a member of an open mule alert in the alert queue                                                 strong

6.3.2 Session integrity & device trust P

Builds on the existing DeviceProfiler (device count, sharing, emulator flags) and adds what happens in the session. The stolen-credentials case in the brief is caught by SD06–SD08 and
SD10 even when the device is the customer's own.

Code  What it checks                                                                                                                      Kind
                                                                                                                                          weak
SD01  device never seen for this customer                                                                                                 strong
                                                                                                                                          strong
SD02  device used by 3 or more other customers                                                                                            strong
                                                                                                                                          strong
SD03  emulator, rooted or automation flags in the fingerprint                                                                             strong (device) · weak (IP only)
                                                                                                                                          strong (0.85)
SD04 / SD05 IP on the Tor exit list / inside a Spamhaus DROP block (real lists)                                                           strong
                                                                                                                                          weak
SD06  impossible travel: faster than 900 km/h since the last session

SD07  device changed inside the same session (an IP change alone is weak context: mobile networks)

SD08  takeover sequence within 30 min: credential, SIM or phone-number change → login from a new device or place → payee added → payment

SD09  remote-access tool active during the payment

SD10  typing rhythm far from the customer's own (only when the app supplies it)
6.3.3 Payment intent & beneficiary intelligence P

Looks at where the money is going and why. It reads the mule core's ring map for one-hop links but leaves ring membership itself to MN02. It connects the existing ScamVictimDetector ,
which has no API today. The detector's age-based signal (SV05) is not used, because age is context and never evidence.

Code         What it checks                                                                             Kind
BI01         payee is a known-fraud account: confirmed through feedback, or named in a fraud complaint  decisive
BI02         payee sent to or received from members of a flagged ring (one hop)                         strong
BI03         payee received from 5 or more different new payers in 24 h                                 strong
BI04         payee first seen less than 7 days ago                                                      weak
BI05 / BI06  first payment to this payee / payee added less than 30 min ago                             weak / strong
BI07         customer on a call or being coached during the payment (scam detector SV01, SV02)          strong
BI08         scam phrases in the payment note (scam detector SV06)                                      strong
BI09         collect request or QR payment to an unknown payee                                          weak
BI10         payee name matches the UN sanctions list (real list, fuzzy match ≥ 0.85)                   decisive

6.3.4 Adaptive customer behaviour P

Keeps a small profile per customer, updated after every committed payment: amount statistics on a log scale (median and spread), the hours they pay, their payees with counts and usual
amounts, their devices and places. It answers two questions: is this unusual for this person, and how familiar is this payment.

Code         What it checks                                                                             Kind
BH01         amount far above the customer's own normal (robust z ≥ 3 and above their 99th percentile)  strong, scaled
BH02         more payments in the last hour than 3× the customer's usual busiest hour                   strong
BH03         an hour this customer almost never uses (under 2 % of their history)                       weak
BH04         a city this customer has not paid from before                                              weak
Code             What it checks                                                                                                                        Kind
FC01             familiar payee: 3 or more earlier payments, and this amount within 3× of the usual to them                                            familiarity
FC02 / FC03      known device and usual place / usual hour                                                                                             familiarity

Confidence grows with history: min(1, payments ÷ 30) × min(1, days ÷ 14). A new customer has low confidence, which pushes uncertain cases to step-up rather than review.

6.4 False-positive control

The rule of the platform: one weak or unusual thing never interrupts a customer, and one strong thing on its own never holds a payment. Holding needs agreement between
independent engines or a confirmed fact.

Control          How it works                                                                                                                 Example

Familiarity      Weak reasons are multiplied by (1 − familiarity). Strong and decisive reasons are not.                                       Customer A pays ₹80,000 to the payee
dampening                                                                                                                                     they pay every month: the new-device and
                                                                                                                                              odd-hour reasons fade.

Evidence before  REVIEW needs at least one reason that is evidence of fraud: a mule link (MN01–MN03), a compromised session (SD02–SD09),      Customer B's ₹80,000 to a seller added 12
a hold           or a known-fraud, mule-linked, coached or sanctioned payee (BI01–BI03, BI07, BI08, BI10). New payee, big amount, burst, odd  minutes ago is verified with the customer,
                 hour or city, new device and typing rhythm are context only. Without evidence the score is capped at 69 (STEP_UP).           not held.

Victims are not  Accounts that only paid into a ring are excluded from ring membership (6.3.1).                                               A takeover victim's later everyday
mules                                                                                                                                         payments are not held as "ring member"
                                                                                                                                              payments.

Mobile networks An IP change inside a session is weak context (phones move between Wi-Fi and mobile data). A device change inside a session A festival shopping session that hops

                 stays evidence (SD07).                                                                                                       networks is approved.

Corroboration REVIEW needs at least two engines at 0.4 or more. Otherwise the score is capped at 69 and the band is STEP_UP.                  A big payment from a new phone with no
                                                                                                                                              other evidence asks for re-confirmation; it
                                                                                                                                              is not held.

Decisive facts   MN02, BI01 and BI10 set a floor of 85 and REVIEW, whatever the familiarity.                                                  A long-standing payee who is now in a
                                                                                                                                              confirmed mule ring is still held.
Control       How it works                                                                                                                   Example
Confidence    REVIEW with confidence under 0.35 becomes STEP_UP unless a decisive fact is present.                                           A two-day-old customer with an unusual
                                                                                                                                             amount is asked to confirm.
Anomaly ≠ fraud Behaviour reasons describe "unusual for you". The text says so, and they never reach REVIEW alone.                           A festival purchase spree is a step-up at
                                                                                                                                             most.
Step-up, not  STEP_UP means extra verification: payee name shown, PIN re-entry, a cooling-off warning for large first payments (D03).        —
block
Feedback      A confirmed false alert adds the payee, device and place to the customer's trusted set. Confirmed fraud adds the payee to the  The same false alert does not repeat for
              known-fraud list (BI01).                                                                                                       that customer.
Separate      FPR, the share of genuine payments stepped up, and the REVIEW-band precision are reported separately from recall.              6.12
measurement

6.5 Scoring, bands and the model

          Engine score: noisy-OR of its reasons, 1 − Π(1 − strength), after familiarity dampening.
          Features for the model (10): the four engine scores, familiarity, the number of engines at 0.4 or more, new payee, new device, the amount ÷ the customer's median on a log scale, and
          confidence.
          Model: L2 logistic regression. Each feature's contribution is weight × value in log-odds, so the explanation is exact. Weights are trained on the calibration window of the labelled synthetic
         set and stored as reviewed JSON. They are adopted only if they pass a gate against the hand-set prior on the later test windows of three populations ( scripts/evaluation/
         evaluate_unified.py ). A gradient-boosted comparison model was not built in this stage (D05, remaining work).
          Score = round(100 × probability), then the false-positive controls above.
          Bands (configurable): APPROVE below 40 · STEP_UP 40–69 · REVIEW 70 and above. A DECLINE band exists but is switched off unless the bank's policy turns it on (D03).
          Explanation: the band, the score, the confidence, each engine's contribution in points, the reasons in plain language, and "why not higher" (for example "one engine only: verify, don't
          hold").
6.6 The main request path

payment switch                  Nginx                MuleCatch API  PostgreSQL                                                                        graph refresher

① POST /risk/assess

                                ② mTLS · rate limit

⑤ 200 · score · band · reasons                       ③ 4 engines in RAM:
                                                        profile + ring map

                                                     ④ queue row (non-approved)

                                                                                                                           ⑥ every 2 s: new ring map
                                                                                                                           ⑦ writes hourly rollups

Time: steps ① to ⑤ are in memory; the database write is buffered and flushed every 100 ms, so it never sits on the switch's path.
Duplicates: a repeated txn_id returns the stored decision without touching the profile.
Untrusted callers: POST /risk/assess from staff scores without changing profiles ("what-if"). Only the trusted path ( POST /risk/events , ADMIN role today; a dedicated service role
over mTLS for the switch is proposed) commits. The existing mule scorer follows the same rule.
Dashboards: the decision feed and metrics read the log and the rollups, never the hot path.
6.7 Data model                            Payment event                        Risk decision                 Mule ring / alert

         Customer profile                 txn_id · payer · payee · ₹           score · band · confidence     members · evidence · purity
                                          session · context · source           engines · reasons · model     alert_id · status · STR
              customer_id · amount stats
              payees · devices · places

                                          Outcome + audit                      Payee intelligence

                                          fraud · false alert · by · at        first seen · new payers 24 h
                                                                               known-fraud flag

IDs: decision_id is a random UUID; txn_id comes from the switch and is unique.
History: decisions are append-only; an outcome is a separate dated record, and every outcome also goes into the existing audit hash chain.
Profiles are derived data: they can be rebuilt from the last 60 days of committed events, so they need no backup of their own.
Restricted data: raw fingerprints and typing rhythms are reduced to flags and one number per session before storage; IP addresses are kept only on non-approved decisions.
Migration: the two new tables are added by the existing additive ensure_tables step under its advisory lock; no existing table changes.

