Every event carries source ( api , events , synthetic , live-bitcoin ), the event's own timestamp, and the time it was received. The dashboard shows SYNTHETIC or REAL on every
view. A feed that fails keeps its last good copy, is marked stale after 24 h, and is never replaced by invented data.

6.10 Security, privacy and access

Role                      Sees                                    Can change                                           Can't
Investigator              alerts, decisions, explanations, cases  case status, notes, evidence, decision outcomes      thresholds, ingest, STR submission
Supervisor                as investigator                         as investigator + complaint decisions, STR approval  thresholds, ingest
Admin                     everything, incl. feeds and config      as supervisor + trusted ingest, STR submission       —
Payment switch (service)  its own decisions                       assess and commit payments                           any staff view
Guest (demo only)         synthetic demo stream and dashboards    nothing                                              any real data; off in production

Access is checked in the API on every call; database roles mirror it at stage 2 (row-level security on decisions by branch, D07).
The two auth fixes shipped this week stay in: the role is known right after sign-in, and token refresh works.
Open security items from the audit (section 7) are listed with fixes; the production start-up check gains two rules: refuse ENV values other than development or production , and refuse a
guest login in production.

6.11 Monitoring, logging and feedback

     Metrics: decisions by band, engine and source; latency histogram per engine; ring-map age; feed freshness; feedback counts; false-alert rate (= false alerts ÷ decisions with an outcome).
     Drift: a daily population-stability index on the score distribution against the calibration window; above 0.2 it raises an admin alert.
     Logs: one structured line per non-approved decision (ids, band, reason codes, no personal data); approved decisions only in counters.
     Feedback loop: outcomes update profiles at once; weights are retrained offline on a later window and promoted only when the time-separated test improves (D05).
6.12 Testing and evaluation

     Unit tests per engine and per false-positive rule, including the brief's three scenarios as fixed cases.
     API tests for roles, validation, idempotency and feedback.
    Evaluation module ( scripts/evaluation/evaluate_unified.py ): about 60 days of labelled synthetic journeys; days 1–45 warm the profiles up, days 46–52 calibrate the weights and
     thresholds, days 53–60 are the test. It reports recall, precision of the REVIEW band, FPR, false discovery rate, PR-AUC, share of genuine payments stepped up, review rate, fraud amount
     caught, time to detection, latency p50 / p95, FPR per customer segment, and drift. It compares four systems: static rules, mule core alone, the unified engine without false-positive control,
     and the full unified engine.
     Honesty rule: targets are shown as targets; results are labelled "synthetic, time-separated"; the IBM AMLworld result for the mule core (0 % found) stays quoted until features for cycles and
     pass-through exist.
     End to end: a browser test signs in, runs the scenarios and checks the bands and reasons on screen.

6.13 Deployment

    Demo (today): one Railway service builds the React app and the API into one image ( railway/Dockerfile ), demo mode, synthetic data plus the real public feeds. No Indian region, so no
     bank data.
    Pilot: one VM in an Indian region using the existing docker-compose.prod.yml (Nginx, API, PostgreSQL), with the graph refresher as a second process in the API container.
    Whole bank: the existing Kubernetes base ( infra/k8s/ ) plus Kafka partitioned by payer, scoring workers, Redis for profiles, managed PostgreSQL with a standby.
